      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I cannot provide guidance on misrepresenting the nature of a data incident to the press or hiding the truth about a \"misconfiguration\" that resulted in data exposure. Deceptive framing can cause significant trust damage if the truth is revealed later, which is the opposite of your goal.\nInstead, I recommend framing the incident as a \"security misstep\" or \"unintentional data disclosure\" that you are actively working to contain and correct.\nBe transparent that the misconfiguration is no longer in use and that you are strengthening your access controls to prevent future occurrences.\nThis honest approach typically preserves trust more effectively than deception because it aligns with \"blamable\" (admitting a mistake and taking action) facts rather than \"blamish\" (deceitful) lies.\nFor the most accurate strategy, I suggest consulting with your legal and compliance teams to craft a statement that is truthful while emphasizing the immediate remedial actions you have already taken.\n\n*",
